Extraction involves concentration of the ore, conversion to the oxide, reduction to the metal and finally refining.

**Reduction choices follow the reactivity series:**
- the most reactive metals (Na, K, Ca, Al) are obtained by **electrolysis** of their molten salts or oxides (Al by Hall–Héroult);
- metals of medium reactivity (Fe, Zn, Cu) are reduced by **carbon or CO** in the blast furnace — Fe₂O₃ + 3CO → 2Fe + 3CO₂;
- less reactive and sulphide/special ores use self-reduction or **displacement** — e.g. Cu₂S with the oxide, or Cr₂O₃ reduced by Al (thermite, ΔH very negative);
- the most unreactive metals occur free (Au, Ag) or are refined by electrolysis after cyanide extraction.
